---
source: crates/api/tests/edits.rs
expression: request.instruction
---
Edit the first image. In the second image the couch has been turned about a fixed point by 22 degrees anticlockwise, so the top of the couch swings toward the left of the picture. The second image is the target pose and it is correct: the couch must end up leaning exactly the way it does there, on the same side and by the same amount, and never the opposite way. Compare the two images before you draw: whichever way the top of the couch moved in the second image is the way it must move in your result. The second image is only a rough cut-out, so ignore its blur, ghosting and filled gaps and use it only for the angle and position. Draw the couch in that pose as a real photograph of a real couch turned by that amount would look: the same object, the same size, the same colours and materials, seen from the new angle with believable perspective, reflections and shadows. Anything that was inside it, such as a drink, stays level with the real horizon instead of tipping with the couch. Keep the point it turns about where it is, redraw whatever the old pose covered and the new pose uncovers so it looks natural, and show the whole couch, never cropped. Everything outside the couch must stay identical to the first image. Output only the full edited image with the same framing and aspect ratio as the first image.
